### What is the Permutation in a String problem?

Given a source string `s` and a pattern `p`, determine whether `s` contains any permutation (anagram) of `p` as a contiguous substring. For example, if `s = "EIDBAOOO"` and `p = "AB"`, the answer is true because the substring `"BA"` at index 3 is a permutation of `"AB"`.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// s = "EIDBAOOO", p = "AB"
// Substring "BA" at index 3-4 is a permutation of "AB"
// Answer: true
```

### Why use a fixed-size window?

A permutation of the pattern has exactly the same length as the pattern. So we only need to check substrings of length `|p|`. A fixed-size window of width `|p|` slides across `s`, and at each position we check whether the window's character counts match the pattern's counts.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Pattern "ABC" has length 3
// Only substrings of length 3 can be permutations
// Fixed window of width 3 slides across the source
// One char enters, one char leaves per step -> constant work per slide
```

### How does the matched counter work?

Comparing two full count arrays on every slide costs O(alphabet size) per step. The matched counter avoids this by tracking how many distinct required characters currently have exactly the right count. When a character enters the window and its count reaches the required amount, matched increments. When its count moves away from the required amount (goes one over), matched decrements. The mirror applies when a character leaves.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class PermutationInString {

    public boolean checkInclusion(String p, String s) {
        int pLen = p.length();
        int sLen = s.length();
        if (pLen > sLen) return false;

        int[] need = new int[26];
        int[] window = new int[26];
        for (char c : p.toCharArray()) need[c - 'A']++;

        int required = 0;
        for (int n : need) if (n > 0) required++;

        int matched = 0;

        for (int right = 0; right < sLen; right++) {
            // Add entering character
            int in = s.charAt(right) - 'A';
            window[in]++;
            if (need[in] > 0 && window[in] == need[in]) matched++;
            else if (need[in] > 0 && window[in] == need[in] + 1) matched--;

            // Remove leaving character when window exceeds pattern size
            if (right >= pLen) {
                int out = s.charAt(right - pLen) - 'A';
                if (need[out] > 0 && window[out] == need[out]) matched--;
                else if (need[out] > 0 && window[out] == need[out] + 1) matched++;
                // Wait: the logic is: before decrement, check crossing
                // Actually let me rewrite correctly
            }

            // Remove leaving character (corrected logic)
            if (right >= pLen) {
                int out = s.charAt(right - pLen) - 'A';
                if (need[out] > 0 && window[out] == need[out]) {
                    matched--; // about to drop below required
                }
                window[out]--;
                if (need[out] > 0 && window[out] == need[out]) {
                    matched++; // dropped back to exactly required
                }
            }

            if (matched == required) return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```

Let me provide a cleaner implementation:

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class PermutationInString {

    public boolean checkInclusion(String p, String s) {
        int pLen = p.length();
        int sLen = s.length();
        if (pLen > sLen) return false;

        int[] count = new int[26]; // combined count: need - window
        for (char c : p.toCharArray()) count[c - 'A']++;

        int matches = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < 26; i++) {
            if (count[i] == 0) matches++;
        }

        for (int right = 0; right < sLen; right++) {
            int in = s.charAt(right) - 'A';
            count[in]--;
            if (count[in] == 0) matches++;
            else if (count[in] == -1) matches--;

            if (right >= pLen) {
                int out = s.charAt(right - pLen) - 'A';
                count[out]++;
                if (count[out] == 0) matches++;
                else if (count[out] == 1) matches--;
            }

            if (matches == 26) return true;
        }
        return false;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        PermutationInString solver = new PermutationInString();
        System.out.println(solver.checkInclusion("AB", "EIDBAOOO"));
        System.out.println(solver.checkInclusion("AB", "EIDCAOOO"));
    }
}
```

```text
true
false
```

### How does the combined count array work?

Instead of two separate arrays, you can use a single `count` array initialized to the pattern's frequencies. As characters enter the window, you decrement; as they leave, you increment. When an entry reaches zero, that character has exactly the right count in the window. When all 26 entries are zero, the window is a permutation. Track the number of zero entries with a `matches` counter for O(1) checks per slide.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// count[i] = need[i] - window[i]
// count[i] == 0 means character i has exactly the right amount
// matches == 26 means ALL characters have the right amount
// Characters not in the pattern start at 0 and stay 0 unless they appear in s
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include: pattern longer than source (return false immediately), pattern and source are identical (return true), single-character pattern and source, and pattern with duplicate characters.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Edge case 1: p longer than s -> false
// Edge case 2: p == s -> true
// Edge case 3: p = "A", s = "A" -> true
// Edge case 4: p = "AAB", must find substring with 2 A's and 1 B
// Edge case 5: p = "A", s = "B" -> false
```

### How is this different from Find All Anagrams?

This problem returns true/false on the first match. Find All Anagrams collects every matching start index. The algorithm is identical except that instead of returning on the first match, you add the start index to a list and continue.

| Aspect | Permutation in string | Find all anagrams |
|---|---|---|
| Output | boolean | List of start indices |
| First match | Return true | Add to list, continue |
| Template | Fixed-size window + matched counter | Same |

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Permutation in string: if (matches == 26) return true;
// Find all anagrams:     if (matches == 26) result.add(right - pLen + 1);
// Same algorithm, different output collection
```

### What about a banking use case?

At the National Bank of India, loan files require a specific set of approval stamps in specific quantities but in any order. Rajesh checks whether any consecutive block in the stamp ledger matches the required set.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class ApprovalStampChecker {

    private final PermutationInString solver = new PermutationInString();

    public boolean hasValidApprovalBlock(String stampLedger, String requiredStamps) {
        return solver.checkInclusion(requiredStamps, stampLedger);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ApprovalStampChecker checker = new ApprovalStampChecker();
        // M=manager, A=auditor, S=senior
        System.out.println(checker.hasValidApprovalBlock("XMMSAXASY", "MAS"));
        System.out.println(checker.hasValidApprovalBlock("XMMXAXY", "MAS"));
    }
}
```

```text
true
false
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is comparing full count arrays on every slide instead of using the matched counter, which turns O(n) into O(n * alphabet). Another mistake is not handling the entering and leaving character updates symmetrically, leading to off-by-one in the matched count.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// WRONG: comparing full arrays per slide -> O(n * 26)
for (int i = 0; i < sLen - pLen + 1; i++) {
    if (Arrays.equals(window, need)) return true; // O(26) per step
}

// CORRECT: matched counter -> O(1) per slide
if (matches == 26) return true;
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should know that the matched counter is what separates a clean O(n) solution from a naive O(n * alphabet) one. The combined count array technique (need minus window in a single array) is slightly cleaner than maintaining two arrays because it uses one array and a single zero-check. This same fixed-window template solves the entire family of anagram-search problems.

```java
